Optimal Solution - Top-down DP solution to count coin change combinations using memoisation and unbounded choices.
                   Model it as an unbounded knapsack: choose to take the same coin again or skip to the next, caching results to avoid recomputation.

class Solution {
    // Memoization table: t[i][amt] stores number of ways
    // using coins from index i to make amount = amt
    int[][] t;
    // Number of coins
    int n;
    public int change(int amount, int[] coins) {
        // Total number of coin types
        n = coins.length;
        //Initialise memo table with -1 (uncomputed states)
        t = new int[n + 1][amount + 1];
        for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j <= amount; j++) {
                t[i][j] = -1;
            }
        }
        // Start recursion from index 0 with full amount
        return solve(0, coins, amount);
    }
    public int solve(int i, int[] coins, int amount) {
        // If the exact amount is formed, count one valid way
        if (amount == 0) return 1;
        // If no coins left, but the amount remains
        if (i == n) return 0;
        // Return cached result if already computed
        if (t[i][amount] != -1) return t[i][amount];
        int take = 0;
        // Option 1: take current coin (unlimited times)
        if (coins[i] <= amount) {
            take = solve(i, coins, amount - coins[i]);
        }
        // Option 2: skip the current coin and move to the next
        int skip = solve(i + 1, coins, amount);
        // Store and return total ways
        return t[i][amount] = take + skip;
    }
}

Time Complexity :- O(n * amount).
Space Complexity :- O(n * amount).
